<div class="page-header"><div class="page-header-inner"><h1 class="page-title">Workout Split Methodology</h1><p class="page-desc">The rules behind the Split Finder, explained so you can judge them for yourself.</p></div></div>
<div class="seo-page">

  <h2>How we define a split</h2>
  <p>Each split in SplitsWorkout records how many days it takes, what each day trains, how often each muscle is trained per week, a typical session length, a difficulty level and example exercises. The standard versions are push pull legs, upper/lower, full body, the bro (body-part) split and the Arnold split.</p>
  <h2>How the Split Finder ranks splits</h2>
  <p>The finder is rules-based: no AI model is involved and nothing is sent to a server. It scores each split against your seven answers:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Training days</strong> (largest weight). Every split has an ideal, workable or stretch range of days; splits that do not suit your days are excluded.</li>
    <li><strong>Experience.</strong> Simpler, higher-frequency plans score better for beginners; high-volume plans score better for advanced lifters.</li>
    <li><strong>Goal.</strong> Muscle gain, strength, fat loss and general fitness each favour different structures.</li>
    <li><strong>Session length, preference and recovery</strong> adjust the ranking, for example favouring shorter sessions or dedicated muscle-group days.</li>
    <li><strong>Equipment</strong> nudges the ranking and adds a note, since body-part days rely more on machines and cables.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Every recommendation lists the reasons it scored well and any cautions. We show tiers (best fit, strong alternative, also workable) rather than percentage scores, because a percentage would suggest precision these rules do not have.</p>
  <h2>Why we cap training at six days</h2>
  <p>If you choose seven days, the finder schedules six training days and one rest day. Hard training every day leaves no recovery window, and recovery is where adaptation happens.</p>
  <h2>Principles behind the advice</h2>
  <p>Our advice follows widely accepted resistance-training principles: train each muscle regularly, keep total weekly volume sensible, progress gradually and recover adequately. Where evidence is mixed — for example, the best training frequency — we say so rather than pretending there is one right answer.</p>
  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <p>The finder cannot see your injuries, history, schedule changes or recovery. Treat results as a starting point to try for 8–12 weeks, not a prescription. Try it on the <a href="/workout-split-finder">Split Finder</a> or read the <a href="/workout-splits/best-workout-split">best workout split guide</a>.</p>
</div>
</Layout>
